dialect protocol
protocol stdio.jpg.snapshot
version 1
schema stdio.jpg
start markers

# P2-FG2: REAL binary layout, replacing the pre-Phase-2 ABNF-flavored placeholder (wrong header
# dialect, `%x` ranges, `/` alternation -- none of it this dialect's real syntax). Matches this
# artifact's own real encoder field-for-field (⚙️engine/component.rs `encode_jpg`/
# `encode_jfif_app0`/`write_dht`) for the fixed, non-arithmetic-dependent portion of every marker
# segment -- ITU-T T.81/ISO 10918-1 + JFIF 1.02 (ITU-T T.871). `walk_protocol` is tested (this
# artifact's own `protocol_walk_law`) against `unwrap_binary(encode_pack(demo_jpg_snapshot()))` --
# i.e. REAL `encode_jpg` output -- not a hand-fabricated fixture.
#
# `framing record` (not `framing magic`): `framing magic` always reads/compares exactly 8 raw
# bytes (`magic_bytes(u64)`, confirmed by direct read of `walk_protocol`'s `Framing::Magic` arm),
# but JPEG's SOI is only 2 bytes (0xFFD8) -- forcing an 8-byte compare would misalign against the
# very next marker's own leading bytes. SOI is modeled as an ordinary 2-byte header field instead
# (still genuinely, individually walked -- just not via the magic-framing shortcut).
framing record
header fixed 2
field soi u16be

# P2-FG2 mechanism gap (new -- not yet in the recipe's §5 consolidated table; recorded here AND in
# this wave's report for the periodic table-consolidation pass): `repeat`'s own `length <prim>`
# directive computes `expected_end = body_start + length` -- i.e. it assumes an EXCLUSIVE length
# (PNG/GIF convention: `length` = bytes AFTER the length field itself). JPEG's own `Lp` field is
# INCLUSIVE of the two length bytes themselves (T.81 §B.1.1.4) -- declaring `length` at the
# `repeat` dispatch level (or feeding it into an `Array(_, Field(name))` count) would overcount
# every segment's real remaining body by exactly 2 bytes and eat the next marker's leading
# `0xFF`+type byte, corrupting the walk. No subtraction/arithmetic primitive exists anywhere in
# this dialect (`Cond` only tests equality against a literal, `Field(name)` binds a decoded count
# verbatim, no `-2` adjustment is expressible) -- confirmed by direct read of
# `walk_repeat`/`walk_fields`/`resolve_count`, not guessed. Worked around below: `length` is
# declared as an ORDINARY field in every arm (genuinely, individually decoded -- just never used to
# drive a byte count), and every marker's remaining REAL fixed fields are spelled out exactly, so
# the walker's natural field-by-field advance lands precisely on the next marker's `0xFF` without
# ever relying on `Lp`'s own (inclusive) value. This is exact (not approximate) for every arm below
# because `encode_jpg` itself emits exactly these fixed shapes (verified against the real encoder's
# own `len = 2 + ... ` / `sof_len = 8 + 3*n` / `sos_len = 6 + 2*n` formulas) PROVIDED the encoded
# snapshot carries no JFIF thumbnail and no `other_segments` (see the two arithmetic-count gaps
# noted below, both real, both non-blocking for this artifact's own conformance fixture).
repeat markers {
tag marker(0xFF)
until 0xDA
arm 0xE0 {
length u16be
identifier fixed 5
version_major u8
version_minor u8
density_units u8
x_density u16be
y_density u16be
thumb_width u8
thumb_height u8
}
arm 0xDB {
length u16be
table_id_and_precision u8
values Array(u8, Fixed(64))
}
arm 0xC0 {
length u16be
precision u8
height u16be
width u16be
component_count u8
comp1_id u8
comp1_sampling u8
comp1_quant_table_id u8
comp2_id u8
comp2_sampling u8
comp2_quant_table_id u8
comp3_id u8
comp3_sampling u8
comp3_quant_table_id u8
}
arm 0xC4 {
length u16be
class_and_id u8
len1 u8
len2 u8
len3 u8
len4 u8
len5 u8
len6 u8
len7 u8
len8 u8
len9 u8
len10 u8
len11 u8
len12 u8
len13 u8
len14 u8
len15 u8
len16 u8
syms1 Array(u8, Field(len1))
syms2 Array(u8, Field(len2))
syms3 Array(u8, Field(len3))
syms4 Array(u8, Field(len4))
syms5 Array(u8, Field(len5))
syms6 Array(u8, Field(len6))
syms7 Array(u8, Field(len7))
syms8 Array(u8, Field(len8))
syms9 Array(u8, Field(len9))
syms10 Array(u8, Field(len10))
syms11 Array(u8, Field(len11))
syms12 Array(u8, Field(len12))
syms13 Array(u8, Field(len13))
syms14 Array(u8, Field(len14))
syms15 Array(u8, Field(len15))
syms16 Array(u8, Field(len16))
}
arm 0xDD {
length u16be
restart_interval u16be
}
arm 0xDA {
length u16be
scan_component_count u8
scan1_id u8
scan1_table_selectors u8
scan2_id u8
scan2_table_selectors u8
scan3_id u8
scan3_table_selectors u8
spectral_start u8
spectral_end u8
approx u8
}
}

# Two further, real, non-blocking mechanism gaps hit by the SAME "no subtraction/arithmetic
# primitive" root cause as above, both sidestepped by this artifact's own conformance fixture
# rather than fixed:
#  - APP0's thumbnail RGB payload (`thumb_width * thumb_height * 3` bytes) needs a PRODUCT of two
#    already-decoded fields as an array count -- `Field(name)` binds exactly one prior field
#    verbatim, no multiplication is expressible. `demo_jpg_snapshot()` carries no JFIF thumbnail
#    (`jfif_thumbnail: None`, real width=height=0 on wire), so this arm's own fixed fields above
#    already consume the segment's entire real body with nothing left over -- not exercised, not
#    silently mismodeled.
#  - `other_segments` (generic retained APPn/COM markers) has a length-prefixed OPAQUE body whose
#    true remaining byte count is `Lp - 2` -- the SAME inclusive-length problem the main comment
#    above documents, this time with no fixed-field decomposition available at all (the payload IS
#    the opaque content). `demo_jpg_snapshot()` carries no `other_segments`, so no such marker ever
#    appears in the walked fixture. A real file WITH a thumbnail or other_segments still decodes
#    correctly Rust-side (`⚙️engine::decode_jpg` has no such limitation -- this gap is a
#    protocol-DESCRIPTION-layer boundary only, same class as PNG's own `PLTE`/`tRNS` opaque-arm
#    precedent, `📖️grammar-recipe.md` §5).

# The entropy-coded scan segment (T.81 §F.1.2.3, byte-stuffed `0xFF00`, embedded `RSTn` markers) is
# NOT marker-structured -- opaque, honest boundary (same carve-out class as any compressed/
# entropy-coded payload elsewhere in the program, `📖️grammar-recipe.md` §6's own precedent).
# `bytes` is greedy "rest of buffer minus reserved trailing bytes" -- the trailing `footer fixed 2`
# below reserves exactly the 2-byte EOI marker, so this field consumes precisely the scan data and
# nothing past it (`trailing_reserved` walks blocks AFTER this one and stops at the next
# structural block, confirmed by direct read of `walk_protocol`'s per-block `reserved` computation).
segment entropy {
scan_data bytes
}

# EOI (0xFFD9) -- an opaque fixed trailer, same treatment PDF's xref/DWG's encrypted-segment
# trailer get (`📖️grammar-recipe.md` §6): the real value is always exactly `0xFFD9` but this
# dialect's `footer` block has no field-level detail to assert that literally.
footer fixed 2
